hfswr_tool_v2.zip adds sea clutter (Bragg lines plus second-order continuum, sea states 0–7), skywave interference, ionospheric self-clutter, and a selectable Pd display. With your brief’s single receive dipole, the radar is now clutter-limited at sea state 3 and above: detection range for a 1 m² target falls to about 9–16 km. That is a real consequence of the configuration, not a bug.

What’s new

Detection is now based on Pd. Every range or Pd value comes from signal-to-interference-plus-noise (SINR) after integration. The interference term is noise, skywave interference, sea clutter and ionospheric clutter. You set the required Pd, the Pfa, and Swerling 0 or 1. The defaults (Pd 0.9, Pfa 10⁻⁶) need 13.2 dB.

Sea clutter (Sea clutter tab):

Sea model: a fully developed Pierson–Moskowitz sea, with wave height taken from the WMO sea-state code. Wind direction is selectable.
First-order Bragg lines: Barrick’s standard expression, which reproduces the textbook saturated level of about −17 dB upwind. The lines weaken properly at low sea state and low frequency.
Second-order continuum: scaled to the first-order lines by sea state and frequency.
Clutter cell: range × beamwidth × range resolution. A Doppler dynamic-range floor (default −85 dB) leaks clutter into every Doppler bin.

Skywave effects (Skywave tab):

External interference is added when the frequency is below the 3000 km MUF, using ITU ionospheric maps for the site. Daytime D-layer absorption reduces it. The examples show this halving night-time range at 5 MHz.
Ionospheric self-clutter appears when the frequency is below foF2. It blanks ranges from about the F2 peak height (roughly 280–380 km) outwards. I checked this with a high-power case: 5 MHz detection was cut from 396 km to 320 km.

