Pushback and points to check
The single element is the binding constraint. A 180° beam gathers about 65 dBsm of sea echo per range cell. Even 85 dB of Doppler dynamic range leaves a 1 m² target marginal. Narrowing the beam to 10–15° (a receive array) roughly multiplies the range by 5–8.
Target speed matters a lot. I defaulted the target to 50 m/s radial, which keeps it clear of the sea echo in Doppler. At 10 m/s, sea state 3 and above gives zero detections even with a 15° beam and 60 s integration, because the target sits in the second-order continuum.
Roughness propagation loss is still missing. This is Barrick’s effective-impedance loss, separate from clutter. I couldn’t find a verified source for the integral kernel, so I left it out rather than guess. It will reduce ranges further above about 15 MHz in higher sea states.
Options for next steps
Full Barrick second-order integral. This gives the highest fidelity for slow targets but is a substantial build. The parametric model is probably adequate for fast targets.
Barrick roughness loss. This is the biggest remaining gap at high frequency and high sea state. It needs his 1971 paper (Radio Science, Part 1) for the integral kernel; if you can obtain it, I’ll implement it and check it against the ITU handbook’s 10 MHz curves.
Calibrate the skywave parameters against any spectrum-occupancy or ionosonde data you hold for the South West, such as the Chilton ionosonde.

