Radial-speed chart
What it plots: the radial speed sweeps from 0 to 60 m/s in 0.25 m/s steps by default; negative speeds mean the target is opening. The sweep limits are on the Display tab and can also be set with --vmin/--vmax/--vstep. The month, hour and SSN are the same as the other charts use.
Bragg markers: in range mode the chart shades the first-order Bragg speeds, 2.8–6.8 m/s across 5–30 MHz. In the Pd modes it draws dotted lines at the Bragg speed for the selected frequency.
What it shows (medium confidence): with your single-element defaults at sea state 3, a 1 m² target needs roughly 16 m/s at 30 MHz and 32 m/s at 5 MHz to clear the sea echo. Lower frequencies need more speed because each m/s gives less Doppler shift.
Wind direction matters: with a south-westerly wind, opening targets do better than closing ones. The Bragg line from closing waves is the stronger one.
Reading a zero: a value of 0 km means the target isn’t detected even at the 5 km minimum range. The chart therefore jumps from 0 straight to about 5 km.
