The new Atmospheric noise control is in hfswr_tool_v3_2.zip, on the Path & noise tab (and --atmos tables|parametric for headless runs). It offers ITU-R P.372 tables (the default, unchanged) or Parametric (approx.). The parametric option tracks the tables closely: detection ranges typically agree within ±2 % with Rural man-made noise and ±5 % with Quiet rural.

How the parametric model works
Form: I used the same structure as P.372. The noise level at 1 MHz is a smooth function of local time and month (a 21-term Fourier series). The noise at each frequency is then a linear function of that level, with cubic coefficients in log frequency. The decile spreads are also smooth functions.
Coefficients: fitted by weighted least squares to the P.372 tables for Land’s End, over all months and hours from 3 to 30 MHz. The weighting puts most effort where atmospheric noise dominates the total.
Behaviour: it is smooth through the day, so it doesn’t show the step changes that the tables’ 4-hour time blocks produce. The comparison plot shows this.
Accuracy against the tables (high confidence; measured at this site)
Measure	Rural	Quiet rural
Total median noise, rms error	0.4 dB	1.3 dB
Total median noise, 95th percentile	1.0 dB	2.8 dB
Detection range, rms difference	2.3 % (max 9 %)	5.0 % (max 18 %)

Atmospheric noise on its own is only good to about 5 dB rms. The large errors are mostly above 20 MHz, where atmospheric noise is far below man-made and galactic noise and doesn’t affect the result.

Safeguards
Warnings: you get a warning if the parametric option is used outside 3–30 MHz, or at a site more than 5° of latitude or 10° of longitude from Land’s End. The fit is specific to this site.
Refitting: fit_parametric_atmos() refits the coefficients for another site. Run against Land’s End, it reproduces the embedded values exactly.
CSV record: saved files record which atmospheric option was used, and with the parametric option they include all the coefficients and where they came from.
Plot title: the chart title shows which atmospheric option was used.

I tested both options headless and in the GUI on a virtual display.

Pushback

The parametric option doesn’t remove the dependence on ITU data. It is fitted to the P.372 tables, so it can’t be better than them and is only valid near Land’s End. Its real value is speed and smoothness; the tables are already fast here. I’d keep the tables as the reference for formal results.

